---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import d2028 from '../data/2028.json';
import metaJson from '../data/meta.json';
import ruleJson from '../data/rule.json';

const d: any = d2028, meta: any = metaJson, rule: any = ruleJson;
const f = d.featured, t = d.tally, w = t.wordings;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (x: number, dp = 1) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(dp)}%`;
const noise = pct(meta.headline.p_perfect_rule_under_noise.T1, 1);
const longDate = new Date(`${d.election_day}T12:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { weekday: 'long', year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const verdictNoWin = f.branches.every((b: any) => !b.prediction_hold_party_wins);
const sky = f.cells.filter((c: any) => c.kind === 'sky');
const failing = sky.filter((c: any) => c.state === 'false');
const deg = (x: number) => `${x.toFixed(2)}°`;
const verdictLabel: Record<string, string> = { no_win: 'says no win', win: 'says win', pending: 'undecided (depends on a game not yet played)' };
const share = (n: number, tot: number) => (tot ? n / tot : 0);
const rows1 = [
  { k: 'no_win', label: 'Say no win', n: t.counts.no_win },
  { k: 'win', label: 'Say win', n: t.counts.win },
  { k: 'pending', label: 'Undecided', n: t.counts.pending },
];
const rows2 = [
  { k: 'no_win', label: 'Say no win', n: w.no_win },
  { k: 'win', label: 'Say win', n: w.win },
  { k: 'pending', label: 'Undecided', n: w.pending },
];
---
<Base title="2028" description="What the rule says about 2028, inside the same frame as its disclaimer." current="2028">
  <h1>What the rule says about 2028</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The next presidential election is on {longDate}. The rule can be read for it today, because three of its four facts are
    about the sky. The reading is below, in the same frame as the reason to ignore it.
  </p>

  <section class="frame" aria-labelledby="frame-head">
    <div class="frame-head" role="note">
      <h2 id="frame-head">This is spurious</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>This reading predicts nothing.</strong> The rule fits 1900&ndash;2024 because the search that found it was
        large enough to find a perfect fit in about <strong>{noise}</strong> of histories whose winners had been shuffled. A
        rule found that way carries no information about 2028, and neither does this page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="frame-body">
      <p class="verdict">
        {verdictNoWin
          ? <>Read literally, the rule says <strong>the party holding the White House on {longDate} does not win.</strong></>
          : <>Read literally, the rule&rsquo;s answer for {longDate} <strong>depends on a game not yet played.</strong></>}
      </p>
      <p>
        {verdictNoWin && <>That answer is already settled, whatever happens in the Super Bowl. </>}
        The rule has no inputs about who is running, who is ahead, or who holds the office. &ldquo;The party holding the
        White House&rdquo; is whichever party that turns out to be.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">Sky facts computed {meta.built_utc_date} with {d.ephemeris}. Same conventions as the 1900&ndash;2024 table on the rule card.</p>

      <h3>The four facts</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="plain">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Fact</th><th class="r">Measured</th><th class="r">Line</th><th class="r">Gap to the line</th><th class="r">Gap in clock time</th><th>Fact holds?</th><th>Win needs it to</th><th>Needed state?</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {f.cells.map((c: any) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{c.gloss.replace(/^NOT: /, '')}</td>
                {c.kind === 'sky' ? (
                  <>
                    <td class="num">{deg(c.measured_deg)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{deg(c.threshold_deg)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{c.base_truth ? 'over by ' : 'short by '}{deg(Math.abs(c.margin_deg))}</td>
                    <td class="num">about {fmt(Math.round(c.margin_hours))} h</td>
                    <td>{c.base_truth ? 'yes' : 'no'}</td>
                  </>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    <td class="num" colspan="4">pending: {c.event}</td>
                    <td>pending</td>
                  </>
                )}
                <td>{c.negated ? 'not hold' : 'hold'}</td>
                <td>{c.kind === 'sky'
                  ? ((c.negated ? !c.base_truth : c.base_truth) ? <span class="badge ok">met</span> : <span class="badge flag">not met</span>)
                  : <span class="badge">pending</span>}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sub">
        &ldquo;Line&rdquo; is the threshold in the fact, a difference in ecliptic longitude. &ldquo;Gap in clock time&rdquo; is how far the
        instant would have to move for the measurement to reach the line, from the local rate of change (a rough figure; the
        degrees are the measurement). All gaps are many hours, so no timezone convention changes any of the three sky facts.
      </p>

      <h3>The football fact, both ways</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="plain">
          <thead><tr><th>If, in {f.cells.find((c: any) => c.kind === 'event').event}&hellip;</th><th>Steelers fact</th><th>Rule&rsquo;s answer</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {f.branches.map((b: any) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{b.steelers_win ? 'the Pittsburgh Steelers win (pending)' : 'someone else wins, or the Steelers do not play in it (pending)'}</td>
                <td>{b.steelers_win ? 'does not hold for a win' : 'holds for a win'}</td>
                <td>{b.prediction_hold_party_wins ? 'party holding the White House wins' : 'party holding the White House does not win'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sub">
        {failing.length > 0
          ? <>The answer is the same in both rows because {failing.length === 1 ? 'one sky fact is' : `${failing.length} sky facts are`} already unmet: {failing.map((c: any) => c.gloss.replace(/^NOT: /, '')).join('; ')}.</>
          : <>Both sky facts that are not about football are met, so the football result decides.</>}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="rivals">Equally perfect rules, same question</h2>
  <p>
    The rule above is one of {fmt(t.n_rules)} that score all 32 elections right with at most four facts. Each of the others can be read for
    2028 the same way. Only sky facts can be read today; every sports fact for 2028 is still pending, and a rule that depends
    on one stays undecided unless a sky fact has already ruled it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>They do not all agree, but nearly all of them say the same thing.</strong> That is not a sign of anything. These rules are made from
    the same few sky patterns, so they tend to succeed and fail together. They also agreed with one another, and with all 32 elections,
    by construction.
  </p>
  <p class="panel warn">
    <strong>Agreement among these rules is not independent evidence.</strong> Every one of them was selected because it fits the same 32 outcomes, and they share
    most of their facts. Counting how many of them say the same thing for 2028 is counting one result many times, not many confirmations.
  </p>

  <h3>The other {fmt(t.n_others)} rules, one wording each</h3>
  <div class="mini-bars viz" role="img" aria-label={`Of ${t.n_others} other rules: ${rows1.map((r) => `${r.n} ${r.label.toLowerCase()}`).join(', ')}`}>
    {rows1.map((r) => (
      <div class="mini-row">
        <span class="mini-label">{r.label}</span>
        <span class="mini-track"><span class={`mini-fill k-${r.k}`} style={`width:${Math.max(share(r.n, t.n_others) * 100, r.n ? 0.8 : 0)}%`}></span></span>
        <span class="mini-n">{fmt(r.n)} <small>({pct(share(r.n, t.n_others), 1)})</small></span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <details class="viz-table">
    <summary>Data table</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Reading for 2028</th><th class="r">Rules</th><th class="r">Share</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows1.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="r">{fmt(r.n)}</td><td class="r">{pct(share(r.n, t.n_others), 1)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>
  </details>

  <h3>Counting every interchangeable wording</h3>
  <p>
    Many facts have exactly the same yes-or-no pattern across 1900&ndash;2024, so each of those {fmt(t.n_rules)} rules can be worded in many
    equally perfect ways. Those wordings agree on every past election and not on 2028. Counted this way there are
    <strong>{fmt(w.total)}</strong> perfect rules, and the number that say &ldquo;win&rdquo; is not zero.
    {t.alias_changes_verdict > 0 && <> For <strong>{fmt(t.alias_changes_verdict)}</strong> of the {fmt(t.n_others)} other rules, the answer for 2028 depends on which wording you pick.</>}
  </p>
  <div class="mini-bars viz" role="img" aria-label={`Of ${w.total} worded rules: ${rows2.map((r) => `${r.n} ${r.label.toLowerCase()}`).join(', ')}`}>
    {rows2.map((r) => (
      <div class="mini-row">
        <span class="mini-label">{r.label}</span>
        <span class="mini-track"><span class={`mini-fill k-${r.k}`} style={`width:${Math.max(share(r.n, w.total) * 100, r.n ? 0.8 : 0)}%`}></span></span>
        <span class="mini-n">{fmt(r.n)} <small>({pct(share(r.n, w.total), 1)})</small></span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <details class="viz-table">
    <summary>Data table</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Reading for 2028</th><th class="r">Worded rules</th><th class="r">Share</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows2.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="r">{fmt(r.n)}</td><td class="r">{pct(share(r.n, w.total), 2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>
  </details>
  <details class="viz-table">
    <summary>The {t.wording_sensitive.length} rules whose answer depends on the wording</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th class="r">Rank</th><th>Facts as listed (each marked with its state for 2028)</th><th>Answers reachable by rewording</th><th class="r">Wordings</th><th class="r">Say no win</th><th class="r">Say win</th><th class="r">Undecided</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {t.wording_sensitive.map((r: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="r">{r.rank}</td>
            <td>{r.literals.map((l: any) => (
              <div>{l.gloss.startsWith('NOT: ') ? <em>not </em> : ''}{l.gloss.replace(/^NOT: /, '')} <span class={l.state === 'false' ? 'badge flag' : l.state === 'true' ? 'badge ok' : 'badge'}>{l.state === 'pending' ? 'pending' : l.state === 'true' ? 'met' : 'not met'}</span></div>
            ))}</td>
            <td>{r.reachable.map((v: string) => ({ no_win: 'no win', win: 'win', pending: 'undecided' } as Record<string, string>)[v]).join(', ')}</td>
            <td class="r">{fmt(r.wordings.total)}</td><td class="r">{fmt(r.wordings.no_win)}</td><td class="r">{fmt(r.wordings.win)}</td><td class="r">{fmt(r.wordings.pending)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </details>
  <p class="sub">
    Wordings are counted from the rules&rsquo; interchangeable-fact lists in the frontier file. A rule counts as &ldquo;say win&rdquo; only if every fact
    in it holds for 2028 on today&rsquo;s sky; &ldquo;undecided&rdquo; means no sky fact rules it out and a sports fact is still pending.
    {fmt(w.rules_with_a_wording_that_is_not_no_win)} of the {fmt(t.n_rules)} rules have at least one wording that does not say &ldquo;no win&rdquo;.
  </p>

  <h3>The {d.tied_top_key.length} rules tied at the top of the ranking</h3>
  <p>The featured rule is one of these. They tie on every ranking criterion, so none is the better rule.</p>
  <details class="viz-table">
    <summary>Each tied rule&rsquo;s reading for 2028</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th class="r">Rank</th><th>Facts (each marked with its state for 2028)</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {d.tied_top_key.map((r: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="r">{r.rank}{r.featured && <> <span class="badge ok">featured</span></>}</td>
            <td>
              {r.literals.map((l: any) => (
                <div>{l.gloss.startsWith('NOT: ') ? <em>not </em> : ''}{l.gloss.replace(/^NOT: /, '')} <span class={l.state === 'false' ? 'badge flag' : l.state === 'true' ? 'badge ok' : 'badge'}>{l.state === 'pending' ? 'pending' : l.state === 'true' ? 'met' : 'not met'}</span></div>
              ))}
            </td>
            <td>{verdictLabel[r.verdict]}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="notes">Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What would count.</strong> The reading was computed on {meta.built_utc_date}, before the election, from the pipeline fixed by hash
      <code title={meta.hashes.freeze_sha256}>{meta.hashes.freeze_sha256.slice(0, 16)}&hellip;</code>. But the rule was chosen after seeing the results among {fmt(t.n_rules)} equally perfect ones
      (<a href={url('/limitations/#choice')}>how it was chosen</a>), so even a correct call in 2028 would not be a test of anything.
    </li>
    <li>The football fact is about the Super Bowl played in early 2028; nothing about 2028 football is known today.</li>
    <li>
      The rule&rsquo;s answer is about the party holding the White House, not about any candidate or party label. It is only as good as the
      shuffled-history control says it is: {noise} of the time, a search like this finds a rule that fits.
    </li>
    <li>Sky facts use the same code and ephemeris as the 1900&ndash;2024 table; the 2028 values were checked to give the same numbers as the stored pool for all 32 past years.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
